course: dictionary
lesson: 116
title: Adoption: one word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Adoption is a noun, from the verb to adopt. Its main meaning is taking a child into your family by law, so that you become their parent for ever. «Adoption is made official by a judge in a family court.» The judge makes it official, so the family is legal and permanent.
explain: Adoption can also mean taking an animal from a shelter to give it a home, which is called pet adoption. It can mean starting to use a new idea, rule or method, like the adoption of a new timetable at school. Look at the words around adoption to find which meaning fits.
explain: Context clues help you choose. Words like child, family, judge and parent point to the first meaning. Words like shelter and pet point to the second. Words like timetable and plan point to the third. Always swap your meaning into the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Be kind when you talk about adoption. «If someone asks about your adoption or foster care, you share only what you want to share; your story is yours to tell or keep.» Some families celebrate the day an adoption became official, sometimes called «Gotcha day» or «family day».
fact: Adoption means taking a child into your family by law and becoming their parent for ever.
fact: Adoption can also mean taking an animal from a shelter to give it a home: pet adoption.
fact: The adoption of a new timetable means starting to use a new plan.
fact: Adopted children are as much part of their family as any other child.
fact: Some families celebrate the day an adoption became official, sometimes called «Gotcha day».
exercise: «Adoption is made official by a judge in a family court.» Who makes an adoption official? | A teacher at school | A chef in a kitchen | *A judge in a family court | A dog from the shelter
exercise: A school says it will try the adoption of a new timetable next term. What does adoption mean here? | Giving a pet a home | Taking a child into the school | Cancelling all the lessons | *Starting to use a new plan
exercise: What is the word for taking an animal from a shelter to give it a home? | Timetable | *Pet adoption | Gotcha day | Family court
test: What does «an adopted child» mean? | A child who has a pet dog | *A child who has joined a new family for ever by law | A child who is often ill | A child who has moved to a new school
test: Why might a family celebrate «family day» after an adoption? | Because they must pay a judge | *Because the adoption became official and the child is now part of the family | Because the shelter is closed | Because the timetable has changed
exam: Read this: «If someone asks about your adoption, you share only what you want to share.» What should you do if you do not want to talk about it? | Ask the judge to speak for you | Change your name | *Keep your story private | Tell everyone in the class at once
